Selecting $t$ a Lebesgue point and inserting $\varphi=\rho(t) \in H^1(\mathbb{T}^2)$ in \eqref{eq:for rho initial data}, we get (using H\"older's inequality) $\limsup_{t\to0 } \Vert \rho(t) \Vert^2_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)} \leq \Vert \rho_0 \Vert_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)} \limsup_{t \to0} \Vert \rho(t) \Vert_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)}$; this $\limsup$ is finite since $0\leq \rho \leq 1$ and $\mathbb{T}^2$ is bounded. Thus, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:comparing norms rho t attainment}
   \limsup_{t \to 0^+} \Vert \rho(t) \Vert_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)} \leq \Vert \rho_0 \Vert_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)}. 
\end{equation}
Meanwhile, using $0\leq \rho \leq 1$ we deduce that $\rho(t) \rightharpoonup \rho_0$ weakly in $L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)$; here we also use \eqref{eq:rho attainment old} to identify the limit as $\rho_0$. The weak lower semicontinuity of the norm then implies 
\begin{equation*}
